    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Medieval music spans from fall of Rome through the Black Death to early Renaissance — monophonic chant to early polyphony."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "gregorian-chant",
      "title": "Gregorian chant",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Monophonic, unmeasured sacred Latin song — foundation of Western notation. Neumes evolved into staff notation by Guido d'Arezzo (11th century)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "early-polyphony",
      "title": "Early polyphony",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Organum: parallel voices added to chant. Notre Dame school (Léonin, Pérotin) — 12th-13th century measured rhythm. Motets combined sacred and secular texts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments",
      "title": "Instruments",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lute, harp, vielle (fiddle predecessor), recorder, bagpipe. Minstrels and troubadours performed secular song in courts."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
